hi today I'm gonna talk about Cloud run  health checks a new feature that just  launched in preview you will learn what  they are and why they're useful  first let's look at the life cycle of a  container there's the startup phase when  your container is getting ready to serve  requests and the serving phase when it  actually serves requests  so when does a container transition from  starting to serving  today Cloud run uses a TCP startup probe  as soon as your container listens on its  TCP Port it starts to serve requests  with Cloud run health checks you can set  an HTTP probe and this is useful for  apps that start listening on a DSP Port  before they are ready to serve requests  you can also set a new kind of probe a  liveness probe a liveness probe  determines if a container is still able  to serve requests and if it's not it  will be shut down and Cloud run starts a  new container  you can use liveness probes to stop  containers that get into a corrupt local  state or to force a restart after a  certain amount of time  thank you for listening if you want to  learn more check out my recent talk at  Cloud next in Munich and even if you  don't like containers there are a few  solid dad jokes in there so what do I  say when there's a duck flying into you  check out the dog
 

